Q: Program in GNU GCC Error access database: SQL: TLS/SSL error: SSL is required, but the server does not support it A: <p>In your code: disable SSL (enforce=0) but don't control certificate verification behavior.</p>
<p>The latest version of the mariadb C connector also includes the code required for SSL/TSL connections. Even with SSL not enforced, a certificate verification may be performed if the server offers SSL, potentially causing errors like yours.</p>
<p>You must add:<br />
mysql_options(conn, MYSQL_OPT_SSL_VERIFY_SERVER_CERT, &amp;verify);</p>
<p>This line tells the MySQL client: &quot;When connecting, do not verify (or verify, depending on the value of verify) the authenticity of the server's certificate.&quot; In your code with SSL disabled, this is used to disable verification, making the connection more forgiving even in unexpected situations.</p>
<p>try to modify this part of your code.</p>
<p>Your Code:</p>
<pre><code>    //verify if I use SSL 
    if (SSLConnection == 1) {
        mysql_ssl_set(conn, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
        my_bool enforce = 1;
        mysql_options(conn, MYSQL_OPT_SSL_ENFORCE, &amp;enforce);
        printf(&quot;SSL ON\n&quot;);
    } else {
        my_bool enforce = 0;
        mysql_options(conn, MYSQL_OPT_SSL_ENFORCE, &amp;enforce);
        // Disattiva SSL
        mysql_ssl_set(conn, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

        printf(&quot;SSL OFF\n&quot;);
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Upgrade code with:</p>
<pre><code>if (SSLConnection == 1) {
        mysql_ssl_set(conn, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
        my_bool enforce = 1;
        mysql_options(conn, MYSQL_OPT_SSL_ENFORCE, &amp;enforce);

    } else {
        my_bool enforce = 0;
        my_bool verify = 0;
        mysql_options(conn, MYSQL_OPT_SSL_ENFORCE, &amp;enforce);
        mysql_options(conn, MYSQL_OPT_SSL_VERIFY_SERVER_CERT, &amp;verify); /* Use this option for verify and disable TSL/SSL */

    }
</code></pre>

Q: Assignment operator causes instantiation of constructor template A: <p><strong>Disclaimer:</strong> I know this is tagged <a href="/questions/tagged/language-lawyer" class="s-tag post-tag" title="show questions tagged &#39;language-lawyer&#39;" aria-label="show questions tagged &#39;language-lawyer&#39;" rel="tag" aria-labelledby="tag-language-lawyer-tooltip-container" data-tag-menu-origin="Unknown">language-lawyer</a>. OP asks for a workaround in the comments. I was not able to find a quote in the Standard that explains this, but I got a workaround that keeps (almost) the same syntax.</p>
<hr />
<p>It seems wrapping the incriminating part of <code>Instance</code> into its separate type and have <code>Instance</code> hold an instance of that type makes The Big Thee happy while performing <code>friend</code> injection correctly.
You however then need a deduction guide for <code>Instance</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;auto Identifier&gt;
struct instance_data {
    template&lt;class T, auto=Writer&lt;Identifier, T&gt;{}&gt;
    instance_data(T) { }

    template&lt;auto vv&gt;
    instance_data&amp; operator=(instance_data&lt;vv&gt; const&amp;) {return *this;}
};

template&lt;auto Identifier = []{}&gt;
struct Instance {
    instance_data&lt;Identifier&gt; data;

    template&lt;auto vv&gt;
    Instance&amp; operator=(Instance&lt;vv&gt; const&amp;) {return *this;}
};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
instance_data(T) -&gt; instance_data&lt;[] {}&gt;;

template&lt;auto v&gt;
Instance(instance_data&lt;v&gt;) -&gt; Instance&lt;v&gt;;

//------------------------------------------------------------------------
auto Lambda = [] {};

auto OtherLambda = [] {};

int main() {
    // All of that works
    Instance         first{instance_data{5}};
    Instance&lt;Lambda&gt; second(instance_data&lt;Lambda&gt;{1.2});    
    Instance         third(instance_data&lt;OtherLambda&gt;{1.2});
    Instance         fourth(instance_data&lt;Lambda&gt;{1.2});

    // The assignments work too
    first = second;
    second = third;
    third = fourth;

    // Friend injection is correctly performed
    using stored_type = decltype(Get(Reader&lt;Lambda&gt;{}));
    static_assert(std::same_as&lt;stored_type, double&gt;);
}

</code></pre>
<p>See it live here: <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/59jdc8b4G" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/59jdc8b4G</a></p>
<p><strong>Caveats</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/4E4qYWTb3" rel="nofollow noreferrer">It won't work if you put <code>Reader</code> and/or <code>Writer</code> in a separate <code>namespace of the one of the caller</code></a>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/r6M8G41nd" rel="nofollow noreferrer">But the other things don't matter</a></li>
<li><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/bYaeKqenv" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Proof it will work if you put what's currently in <code>main</code> in the same <code>namespace</code> as <code>Reader</code> and <code>Writer</code></a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/e1bjzbxx9" rel="nofollow noreferrer">You can't give a user-defined constructor to <code>Instance</code>, it must be an aggregate</a></li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>Another obvious workaround is to use a member function <code>assign</code>  that does the job <code>operator=</code> does and keep the rest as-is.</p>
